# Customer Segmentation Analysis

## Overview

This project focuses on analyzing customer characteristics and purchasing behavior to identify meaningful customer segments and support targeted marketing decisions.

The analysis explores four major approaches to customer segmentation:

- Demographic Segmentation
- Behavioral Segmentation
- Value-Based Segmentation
- Lifecycle Segmentation

It also uses the RFM framework to understand customer engagement and value.

## Objectives

- Understand customer characteristics and behavior
- Identify meaningful customer segments
- Analyze customer purchasing patterns
- Identify high-value customers
- Identify customers who may be at risk of becoming inactive
- Support targeted marketing and retention strategies

## Segmentation Methods

### 1. Demographic Segmentation

Customers are analyzed using characteristics such as:

- Age
- Gender
- Income
- Geographic location

### 2. Behavioral Segmentation

Behavioral segmentation focuses on how customers interact with a business.

Key areas include:

- Purchase frequency
- Purchase recency
- Channel preferences
- Engagement patterns

### 3. Value-Based Segmentation

Customers are analyzed based on their value to the business.

Key areas include:

- Customer spending
- Customer lifetime value
- Profitability

### 4. Lifecycle Segmentation

Customers are categorized according to their relationship with the business, including:

- New Customers
- Loyal Customers
- Champions
- At-Risk Customers

## RFM Analysis

The project uses the RFM model to understand customer engagement and value.

| Metric | Description |
|---|---|
| Recency | How recently a customer made a purchase |
| Frequency | How often a customer makes purchases |
| Monetary | How much a customer spends |

RFM analysis helps identify customers based on their purchasing behavior and value.

## Key Customer Segments

### Champions

Customers with high recency, frequency, and value.

### Loyal Customers

Consistent buyers with medium-to-high customer value.

### At-Risk Customers

Previously frequent and high-value customers whose activity is declining.

### New Customers

Recent first-time buyers with low purchase frequency.

